  "preamble": "Loss of the voiced pharyngeal fricative [ʕ] (Semitic ʿayin) without a consonantal trace recurs across Semitic. In Modern Israeli Hebrew ע has merged with א and, like it, is regularly elided, so Tiberian /ʕoˈlɔm/ 'world' is now /(ʔ)oˈlam/. Maltese għ (from Arabic ʕ and ɣ) is no longer pronounced as a consonant and survives only as length on the neighbouring vowel, as in għajn /aːjn ~ ajn/ 'eye'. In Assyrian Neo-Aramaic ʿayin is a marginal phoneme kept mainly in formal or religious speech; in many dialects it is deleted, as in Nineveh Plains [ʔarba] 'four' beside Standard [ʔarbəʕaː]. Akkadian had already lost *ʕ in the 3rd millennium BCE (*ʕaḳrab- 'scorpion' > aqrabum), and Amharic lost it in inherited words (*šabʕ- 'seven' > säbat), unlike Ge'ez and Tigrinya.",
